{"type":"message","id":"357e74e5","parentId":"40b633ad","timestamp":"2026-10-28T04:47:30.530Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Agave installed and anchor-cli is compiling. While that builds, I'm confirming the deploy blocker is real—no funded keypair and the faucet is closed—so I'll go ahead and design the soulbound program now, keeping the Anchor workspace structure compact and straightforward.\n\nFor testing, I'm leaning toward writing a Rust LiteSVM integration test rather than a TypeScript one, since the ticket allows Rust 0.18 and it avoids the complexity of Anchor's newer TS client ecosystem. I'm sketching the initialize_character instruction, laying out the needed accounts—owner, config PDA, asset keypair, mint, ATAs, and the relevant programs—and now working through the CPI details for the Core CreateV1 call with attributes.\n\nFor the mint setup, I'm detailing the Token-2022 flow: creating the mint account sized for the NonTransferable extension, initializing the extension and mint itself with the config PDA as mint authority, creating the associated token account, minting a single token, then revoking mint authority so the token becomes fixed.\n\nFor the trait data, I'll pass a `genesis_traits: String` instruction argument (capped at 128 chars) to store as an attribute rather than trying to build a separate trait vector type.\n\nI need to decide how the config PDA stores fields like owner, character_id, runtime_signer, memory_epoch, life_stage, asset, receipt_mint, and bump — using a simple enum like LifeStage::Born for the stage rather than raw u8 constants. For duplicate rejection, I'll just rely on the native \"already in use\" failure from `init` on the PDA rather than building a manual check, since `init_if_needed` doesn't fit here and the test only needs to assert failure on a second attempt.\n\nNow I'm thinking about file layout: the Cargo workspace at programs/soulbound, with the program crate underneath, and